# assets/ holds the raster sources the image server reads and the
# originals the admin's asset browser uploads. Unlike var/ or lib/,
# this directory MUST stay publicly readable — so the deny below is
# deliberately narrow: it targets only what a webserver might hand to
# an interpreter instead of sending as bytes. Images (jpg, png, webp,
# gif, ico), svg, fonts, pdf and md are untouched.
#
# Why this file exists: the admin upload filter inspects only the LAST
# extension (AssetBrowser::assertExtensionAllowed, via pathinfo), so a
# name like `photo.php.ico` passes as an .ico — and .ico is not
# byte-validated either (only png/jpg/jpeg/webp/gif are). On hosts that
# bind PHP to arbitrary extensions via AddHandler/AddType (a
# shared-hosting classic) such a file would execute. The root .htaccess
# does NOT catch it: its .php rule matches THE_REQUEST followed by a
# space, `/` or `?` — after `photo.php.ico` comes a dot.
#
# Scope, honestly: this stops EXECUTION, not the upload. Such a file
# can still be stored. Reaching the upload form requires an
# authenticated editor, so that is the accepted boundary.
#
# The two mechanisms are NOT equivalent:
#   - `Require all denied` is the one that always works. It refuses the
#     request wherever the suffix sits in the name — `(\.|$)` is the
#     whole point of the pattern.
#   - RemoveHandler/RemoveType only drop an inherited mod_mime binding.
#     Under PHP-FPM (`SetHandler proxy:fcgi`) they do nothing at all.
#     They are here for the mod_mime hosts this finding is about.
#
# Perl/Python/shell suffixes are deliberately NOT in the pattern: `.pl`
# is also a language code, so `banner.pl.jpg` would be blocked for a
# Polish variant — a false positive nobody would trace back to this
# file. The finding is about PHP.

# `.shtml`/`.shtm` are in the list for server-side includes: with
# `Options +Includes` an SSI file can read and emit other files. Only
# the deny below and RemoveType cover it here — a host that wires SSI
# through `AddOutputFilter INCLUDES` would additionally need
# `RemoveOutputFilter`, which is deliberately not set because the deny
# already refuses the request outright.
<IfModule mod_mime.c>
    RemoveHandler .php .php3 .php4 .php5 .php7 .php8 .phtml .phar .phps .pht .cgi .shtml .shtm
    RemoveType    .php .php3 .php4 .php5 .php7 .php8 .phtml .phar .phps .pht .cgi .shtml .shtm
</IfModule>

# `(?i)` is load-bearing, not decoration: <FilesMatch> compares
# case-sensitively while mod_mime treats extensions case-insensitively.
# Without it `photo.PHP.ico` slips past this deny — and it slips past
# exactly where the deny is the only thing that works, namely on a
# PHP-FPM host whose SetHandler pattern is not anchored.
<FilesMatch "(?i)\.(ph(p[0-9]?|tml|ar|ps|t)|cgi|shtml?)(\.|$)">
    Require all denied
</FilesMatch>
